   "point_l1": "The contrastive が and けれども",
   "explain_l1": "が and けれども both join two clauses and flag the second one as unexpected, but they are not interchangeable in tone. が is the neutral default: it is standard in speech, in business email and in academic prose, and it adds no colour of its own. けれども is heavier — it stretches the pause and sounds more deliberate, so it belongs in polite speech and in writing that wants a considered turn rather than a quick one; the casual けど is its everyday descendant. When the contrast deserves a fresh sentence, Japanese usually starts a new one: しかし does that job in formal writing, でも in speech. So the level rises from けど to が to けれども, and the sentence-initial options are a separate decision about how big the turn is.",
