   "explain_l1": "Rendaku is what happens when two words are fused into one: the first consonant of the second element voices, so 手 (て) + 紙 (かみ) is てがみ and not てかみ, and 花 (はな) + 火 (ひ) is はなび. The alternations are k → g, s → z, t → d, and h → b or p (一本 is いっぽん); only that one consonant of the second element changes, never the first. Voicing is blocked when the second element already contains a voiced obstruent — Lyman's law — so 春風 is はるかぜ and 日陰 is ひかげ, because かぜ and かげ already carry a voiced sound. The rule is real but incomplete: 山道 is やまみち with no voicing at all, so the reliable move is to look the compound up and store the reading, rather than to derive it.",
