   "point_l1": "No-adjectives: modifiers that take の, not な",
   "explain_l1": "English gives you no signal for の versus な, so the particle has to be stored with the word. A small, closed set of modifiers takes の: 普通の (普通の人), 特別の (特別の日), 本当の (本当の理由), 本物の. These are the old ナリ-adjectives, and their の forms are the established ones in writing; you will also hear 特別な日, but 大変の and 静かの are simply wrong, because 大変 and 静か are ordinary な-adjectives (大変な一日, 静かな部屋). 同じ is the exception everyone trips on: it modifies a noun with の (同じ人, 同じ話, 同じ動き), so 同じな人 is wrong even though 同じ appears in な-adjective lists; 同じな is only right before an explanatory ending such as ので (同じなので).",
