   "point_l1": "という — quotation and naming",
   "explain_l1": "という does two jobs you have already met. After quoted words it marks what was actually said (「行く」と言った); after a name or a written word it attaches a label, so 「みどり」という店 is a shop called Midori and 「禁煙」という札 is a sign that says no smoking — nobody is speaking in either case. A plain clause plus ということ turns the whole clause into a fact you can hold as a noun: 彼が留学することを知らなかった and 彼が留学するということを知らなかった both mean you did not know he was going abroad, but ということ makes clear that the content is a fact, not an action. というのは takes the same clause and puts it in front as the topic, with the comment after it: 毎日一時間歩くというのは、体にいい. The trap is the English word \"says\": 言う needs a speaker and real words, while という with a name or a definition is closer to \"called\" or \"that is\", so glossing every という as \"says\" turns a shop name into a talking shop.",
