   "point_l1": "Intransitive and transitive pairs",
   "explain_l1": "Many Japanese verbs come in pairs that describe one change from two sides: ドアが開いた reports that it opened, and ドアを開けた reports that someone opened it. The particle is the signal — が marks the thing that changes, を marks the thing being acted on — so learning a pair means learning which member takes which particle. The shapes are systematic: 開く/開ける, 閉まる/閉める, 入る/入れる, 落ちる/落とす, 付く/付ける, 消える/消す, 止まる/止める — the partner is usually built with -eru or -su, which lets you guess a member you have not met yet. Because the transitive side names a doer, it sounds wrong whenever nobody is doing anything.",
