   "point_l1": "大きな/小さな and the particle-less noun phrase",
   "explain_l1": "大きな and 小さな are the attributive forms of 大きい and 小さい: they exist only in front of a noun, never as a predicate. 大きい木 and 大きな木 are both correct, but only 大きい can end a sentence or take です, so 木は大きな and 木は大きなです are impossible. The pair survives because these adjectives split into two shapes — the い-shape you can inflect, and an older な-shape kept for writing and storytelling, the way 同じ and いろんな also work; the difference is register, not size, since 大きな carries a slightly vaguer, more literary feel than 大きい. The second habit is conversational: in speech close to the listener the particles are simply not said — これ、私の。 コーヒー、飲む？ — and the listener supplies the relation from the shared situation. That is familiarity, not laziness; the dropped を or は is still there in the grammar, and you restore it the moment you are talking to someone you are not close to.",
