   "explain_l1": "Part 1 handled the particles that move information around: よ hands over something the listener may not have, ね asks for agreement, な is a comment aimed at yourself. Part 2 handles particles that mark the speaker's stance instead. かな and かしら turn a statement into a question you are asking yourself — かしら is the older, softer-sounding variant. っけ pulls back something you once knew and are no longer sure about. わ, の and さ colour a plain statement as soft, explanatory or casually emphatic, and none of them changes what is being said. The gender labels in textbooks are real but loosening: わ and the explanatory の still lean feminine in many regions, さ leans masculine or young, yet plenty of speakers of both genders use all three, so copy the people you actually talk to rather than a list.",
