   "explain_l1": "You have met んです before, and its written twin のです, so treat this as a reminder: the form changes what a sentence is doing, not when it happened or how polite it is. 国会で決まります reports a fact; 国会で決まるんです presents the same fact as the reason or background for something already on the table. In a question, んですか asks the listener to supply that background — どうして遅れたんですか wants an account of what happened, while 遅れましたか is a yes/no check. The pattern 〜んですけど opens a topic or a request and then leaves it unfinished, which is why it is the standard soft way to ask someone for something.",
